{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on the promotion implementation at exact head 8b112bb7e07924003b7bad448b900e0c5f5221c6, against request 7f89e557cf46bf657122383e9a9e402f9e113f06 and promise f5c5e8d9dd5db10594fdd5ae49ba24a6846e990e. Not merged.\n\nOne defect, and it is small, but it is the kind that matters most on the head that becomes a project's first impression: **`make test` fails at this exact head whenever the checkout path traverses a symlink.**\n\n`TestModuleRootFromRepositoryAndSpike`, new in this promotion, fails with `moduleRoot() from /tmp/prom = /private/tmp/prom, want /tmp/prom`. On macOS `/tmp` is a symlink to `/private/tmp`, and any checkout behind a symlink reproduces it. I confirmed it is not a promotion regression before reporting: the same exact head run from a non-symlinked path passes cleanly, fourteen packages, so this is the new test rather than the moved code.\n\nThe cause is not `moduleRoot`, which is fine. It is `os.Getwd()`: Go answers from `$PWD` when that is valid, so the first call returns the unresolved path the test runner was given, and after `os.Chdir` it falls back to the syscall and returns the resolved one. The test compares the two. Production never chdirs mid-run, so nothing ships broken — but a contributor who clones into a symlinked path, which on macOS includes anything under `/tmp` and several common home-directory setups, gets a red suite on their first `make test` of a freshly public repository. Resolving both sides with `filepath.EvalSymlinks` before comparing closes it.\n\nEverything else I checked is sound, and the shape of the change is right. The diff against 02f56a5c8f05e4ac0421b1c677aaf5b6e6a0492d is forty-six renames, one delete and two creates — the delete is `go.work`, the creates are the two new tests — so this is a move, not a rewrite, and rename detection says so rather than my reading it. The module is `github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq` at the root, `spike/go.mod` is gone, and `spike/` correctly retains only the evidence commands.\n\nI went looking specifically for a path that still resolves for the wrong reason, and did not find one. The two remaining `gitseq/spike` references in `gitseq-report` are both correct: one names `spike/cmd/gitseq-spike`, which genuinely still lives there, and the other is the substring the new `TestEvidenceDefinitionsUsePromotedModulePaths` uses to *forbid* the old layout. The evidence definitions themselves all name `gitseq/internal/...`. The UI embed resolves at `internal/service/uidist` with assets present.\n\nThe layout tests have teeth, and one of them taught me something I should record. The required-path check is mutation-sensitive — dropping `cmd/gs/main.go` fails `TestShippingLayout`. The forbidden-path check came back green under mutation, which looked like a finding until I remembered what it is: an absence assertion passes when the thing is absent, so deleting it also passes, and green there means nothing on its own. The real check is whether it fires, so I recreated `spike/internal/kernel` and it failed as it should. I nearly reported a working guard as broken, which is the inverse of the mistake I have been reporting to others all session.\n\nGates from a non-symlinked path: vet, test across fourteen packages, build, `go test -race`, and `git diff --check` all clean.\n\nTo close: make the new module-root test symlink-stable. Nothing else blocks, and I would be comfortable approving the next head on that one change.","body":{"branch":"task/promote-layout","head":"8b112bb7e07924003b7bad448b900e0c5f5221c6","verdict":"CHANGES-REQUESTED"}}